{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "si-07-comparing-surds-common-order",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Comparing Surds by Common Order",
    "description": "Compare mixed roots exactly by giving them the same root order instead of estimating decimals.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build from same-order comparisons to square-vs-cube roots and then several mixed orders. Every comparison is converted to a common order so the final decision comes from ordinary integer radicands, not calculator approximations.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 9, Surds and Indices. Approved scope: Teach the chapter method used to compare fourth, square and cube roots: take the LCM of the root orders, rewrite each candidate with that common order, then compare the new radicands because equal-order positive roots preserve order. Start with same-order roots, then compare a square root with a cube root, then three or four mixed-order surds. Include negative fractional powers as values between 0 and 1 and source-shaped comparisons such as √2, ∛4, fourth roots and sixth roots. Avoid calculators; the point is to compare integer powers after the order is aligned."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Which is larger: √3 or ∛5?\n\nA decimal calculator can answer it, but the chapter gives a cleaner exam method: make the root orders match. Once both are sixth roots, you only compare integers.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the root order is already the same, comparison is immediate.\n\n√13 is larger than √11 because 13>11.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Positive square root preserves order. The same is true for cube roots, fourth roots, and other positive roots of the same order.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
